# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-16 (filed by panel 084, repaired after it). Symptom: a
# generic function that calls another generic was `heroes check` exit 0 and
# `heroes build` **exit 2** — `internal error: compiling the generated C failed:
# error: too many arguments to function call, expected 0, have 1`, pointing at
# `t2 = hero_unreachable(t1);`, a line the compiler wrote.
#
# Cause, two halves. `Checked::instantiations` is keyed by `span.start`, which is
# exact at check time — a span occurs once — and stale after monomorphisation,
# where the same span occurs once **per copy of the enclosing body** while the
# table still holds the template's unsubstituted `[#0]`. The emitter read that
# table, found no function matching `[#0]`, and fell back to the literal NAME
# `hero_unreachable`, handing arguments to a zero-argument `_Noreturn`.
#
# Fix, also two halves. The answer moves onto the copy: `Function::call_instances`
# is per-instance and substituted, so each copy knows which callee its inner calls
# reach. And the fallback goes in the loud direction (CLAUDE.md §11) — a missed
# lookup is the compiler being wrong, so it emits `hero_unreachable()` with no
# arguments and says so, instead of writing C that cannot compile.
#
# Why it survived: of the four goldens with two or more generics, EVERY call was
# from `main`. Nothing composed. §1.11's Tier 2 — `map`, `filter`, `fold`, `find`,
# `any`, `all` — is a library that composes, so the port would have met this.
# Named after the defect, per Go's `test/fixedbugs` (CLAUDE.md §9).
#
# The shapes attacked around it, all measured before this case was written: three
# levels deep at three different element types; one generic calling two different
# generics at two types; and polymorphic recursion, which must STILL be refused
# and is (`polymorphic_recursion`, exit 1).

record P
    x: i64

function innermost<C>(zs: [C]) -> C
    return zs[0]

function middle<B>(ys: [B]) -> B
    return innermost(ys)

function outer<A>(xs: [A]) -> A
    return middle(xs)

function tally<D>(ds: [D]) -> i64
    return len(ds)

function both<E>(es: [E]) -> i64
    return tally(es) + tally([outer(es)])

function main()
    # One chain, three element types — each needs its own copy of all three.
    print(outer([7, 8]))
    print(outer(["ziggy", "stardust"]))
    p = outer([P(x: 5), P(x: 6)])
    print(p.x)
    # And one generic reaching two different generics, at two types.
    print(both([1, 2, 3]), " ", both(["only"]))
